    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built two lines of fortifications round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a>, an inner one against the besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> and an outer one against the relief force, and the Gauls' combined attacks on them failed.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s figure of a quarter of a million for the relief force is probably an exaggeration; modern historians usually think a number between 50,000 and 100,000 more credible.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a> was the last major engagement between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and the end of generalised, organised resistance, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> did not become a Roman province until 27 BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alise-sainte-reine">Alise-Sainte-Reine</a> in the 1860s, funded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, uncovered evidence of Roman camps, and a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> was then dedicated at the site.</p></aside>
    </section>

