    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">58 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> becomes proconsul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a>, Illyricum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a> and begins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">53 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> announces that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> is to be treated as a Roman province</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">52 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> unites a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> and revolts, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaricum">Avaricum</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">52 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> is defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a> and then beats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>'s cavalry at the Vingeanne</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 52 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> besieges <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a>, fends off the relief force and receives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>'s surrender</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">49 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> crosses the Rubicon, beginning the Roman Civil War</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">46 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> is paraded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s triumph and garrotted</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">27 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Gallic territories north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a> become a Roman province under Augustus</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-alesia" data-v="aef0eac112" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Two lines of fortification round Alesia</b> Caesar shut the Gauls into Alesia and built two lines of works, one facing the town and one facing outwards against a relief force. The Roman lines, with a camp of two legions on a hill beside them, stood ready.</li><li><b>The relief force and the besieged attack together</b> The relief force arrived and attacked the outer line while the besieged Gauls attacked the inner line at the same moment. The combined attack failed.</li><li><b>A night attack fails and the Gauls withdraw</b> The Gauls attacked again at night. The relief force withdrew at first light for fear of being surrounded, and the besieged, delayed by filling the Roman trenches, returned to the town.</li><li><b>Sixty thousand Gauls attack the weak camp</b> The Gauls chose a weak point where a camp of two legions stood on bad ground and sent 60,000 men against it, attacking at noon. Vercingetorix sallied against the inner line at the same time.</li><li><b>Labienus and Caesar come to the weak point</b> Caesar sent Labienus to support the weak area, then led fresh troops there himself. The attack on the inner line was repelled.</li><li><b>Roman cavalry takes the Gauls in the rear</b> Part of the Roman cavalry left the outer line and appeared at the rear of the Gauls. The Roman troops advanced rapidly, the Gauls fled, and the cavalry intercepted them. The besieged Gauls were pulled back from the fortification.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_(26724093101)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 58 BCE, after his first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> in 59 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> was appointed proconsul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a>, Illyricum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>. A proconsular term was meant to last one year, but his was set at an unprecedented five, and he commanded four legions. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a> (58–50 BCE) he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 55 BCE, intervened in disputes among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> in 57 BCE and marched on the Belgae. From then on he conquered the Gallic peoples one by one. His successes brought him political prestige in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and great wealth through the spoils of war and the sale of war captives as slaves.</p><p>The campaigns of 53 BCE were harsh, and the Gauls feared for their prosperity. In that year Caesar announced that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> was to be treated as a Roman province, subject to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman laws</a> and religion. The Gauls feared that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> would destroy their holy land, watched over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnutes">Carnutes</a>, where the druids met each year to mediate between the tribes. The threat to their sacred lands united peoples who had not been united before. Over the winter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>, the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>, assembled an unprecedented coalition of Gauls.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Alesia&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>Catherine Gilliver, Caesar's Gallic wars, 58–50 BC (Routledge, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vercingetorix.jpg" alt="Vercingetorix" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vercing%C3%A9torix_stat%C3%A8re_Gallica_avers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Roman commanders named in the fighting are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>, Marc Antony, Caius Trebonius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Labienus</a>, Brutus and Caius Fabius, and Caesar also had Germanic allies, who pursued the Gallic cavalry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> were led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>, who had avoided open battle that year, raided foraging parties and supply trains, and abandoned oppida he could not hold. After defeat at the Vingeanne he withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a>, an oppidum on a lofty hill with two rivers on two sides, where about 80,000 men are said to have been garrisoned beside the local civilians. He intended Alesia as a trap for a pincer attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and sent for a relief army at once.</p><p>Caesar chose to starve the place out. His contravallation round Alesia was eleven Roman miles (16 km) long with 23 redoubts, and his outer circumvallation, facing the other way, ran for 14 Roman miles (20.7 km). Caesar describes trenches, one filled with river water, a palisaded rampart, turrets every 80 pedes, and hidden pits with stakes and hooked stakes before the lines. Archaeology suggests the lines were not continuous, as he claims, and made much use of the terrain. For the relief force Caesar gives a quarter of a million, probably an exaggeration; modern historians usually think a number between 50,000 and 100,000 more credible.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> sallied on horseback while the works were going up, and before they were finished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> sent out all his cavalry by night to rally the tribes. When the relief force arrived it camped on a hill a mile from the Roman lines, and the next day attacked the outer fortification while the besieged Gauls attacked the inner one. The combined attack failed. The Gauls attacked again by night; Marc Antony and Caius Trebonius brought troops from the remotest forts, and at first light the relief force withdrew for fear of being surrounded. Vercingetorix's men were delayed by having to fill trenches and returned to the town.</p><p>The Gauls then noticed that the north side of a hill could not be included in the Roman works, and a camp of two legions stood there on steep, disadvantageous ground. They chose 60,000 men under Vercassivellaunus, a near relative of Vercingetorix, who marched before dawn and attacked at noon, while Vercingetorix sallied against any weak part of the inner line. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Labienus</a>, Brutus and Caius Fabius with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a>, then came himself with fresh troops. Labienus was close to collapse when Caesar arrived. The Roman cavalry appeared behind the Gauls, who fled and were intercepted and slaughtered. Caesar wrote that the whole enemy force could have been destroyed had the soldiers not been wearied.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Alesia&#8221; cites where it covers Siege, Battle:</p><ul><li>Catherine Gilliver, Caesar's Gallic wars, 58–50 BC (Routledge, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the repeated failures the relieving army melted away. The next day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> called the Gallic council and proposed that he be killed or surrendered alive to appease the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> to give up their weapons and deliver their chieftains, and Vercingetorix was surrendered. The captives were given to the Roman soldiers as spoils of war, apart from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>, whom Caesar hoped to win over. Earlier in the siege the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a> had sent out their old, sick, wives and children to save food, hoping Caesar would take them in; he forbade it, and they were left between the lines to starve.</p><p>Caesar set his legions to winter in the lands of the defeated tribes. South-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> was not yet pacified and there were mopping-up operations in 50 BCE. Vercingetorix was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for five years, paraded in Caesar's triumph and ritually garrotted in the Tullianum in 46 BCE. The Senate declared 20 days of thanksgiving but, for political reasons, refused Caesar a triumphal parade. Gaul was not formally made into provinces until the reign of Augustus in 27 BCE, and there may have been unrest as late as 70 CE.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Alesia&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Catherine Gilliver, Caesar's Gallic wars, 58–50 BC (Routledge, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The event is described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> himself in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commentarii-de-bello-gallico">Commentarii de Bello Gallico</a>, and by later ancient authors, namely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>. After 50 BCE Aulus Hirtius took over the writing of Caesar's reports on the war. The only records are Roman and so presumed to be biased, and figures for armies and casualties have always been a propaganda weapon and are suspect. Caesar's quarter of a million for the relief force is probably an exaggeration meant to enhance his victory. His reports elsewhere are doubted too: at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a> his casualty numbers are likely much lower than the real ones, and his claim to have sounded a retreat there is dubious.</p><p>Archaeology bears on the siege works. Modern excavation suggests Caesar's preparations were not as complete as he describes, though it is apparent that the works did their job. For many years the site was unknown. Alaise in the Franche-Comté and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alise-sainte-reine">Alise-Sainte-Reine</a> in the Côte-d'Or were the main candidates, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, who favoured the second, funded excavations in the 1860s that uncovered evidence of Roman camps. Research since then has established Alise-Sainte-Reine as most probably the site, and excavations in the 1990s led Colin Wells to say they should have removed all doubt. Other proposals, such as Chaux-des-Crotenay, are not accepted by the vast majority of the scientific community.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii.jpg" alt="Napoleon III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexandre_Cabanel_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexandre Cabanel, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a> is called the climactic engagement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a>, the last major engagement between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and a classic example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-warfare">siege warfare</a> and investment. It marked the end of Gallic independence in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. Paul K. Davis writes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s victory established Roman dominance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> for the next 500 years and also created a rivalry with the Roman government. Adrian Goldsworthy writes that virtually all the tribes that rebelled capitulated and accepted the reality of conquest. Tom Holland writes that by the Romans' measure of a man's capacity to withstand ordeals of exhaustion and blood, Caesar had proved himself the foremost man in the Republic.</p><p>For Caesar the victory was a large personal success, militarily and politically. Political tension rose, and in 49 BCE he crossed the Rubicon, which precipitated the Roman Civil War of 49 to 45 BCE. He was made dictator for life in 44 BCE. His growing personal power and honours undermined the republican foundations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and civil wars followed his assassination, ending in Octavian's defeat of Marc Antony and the beginning of rule by emperors. Under Agrippa, the first governor of Gaul from 39–38 BCE, a radial road network was built centred on Lugdunum (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> dedicated a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> at the site.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>In September 52 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> and his Gallic coalition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a> behind two lines of fortifications and beat off a large relief force. Vercingetorix surrendered, and organised Gallic resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> ended, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> became a province only in 27 BCE.</em></p>

